          <!-- ── 01 CSR Problem ── -->
          <section id="csr-problem" class="section reveal">
            <p class="eyebrow">01 · The Problem</p>
            <h2 class="sec-title">Why did Angular need SSR at all?</h2>
            <p class="sec-lead">
              Angular started as a framework where <strong>the browser does all the work</strong>.
              Fine in 2016 on fast desktops — but 3 billion people now browse on mobile phones, often on slow networks.
            </p>

            <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:.75rem;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
              {[
                { bg: '#fef2f2', border: '#fecaca', icon: '😩', title: 'Users on slow phones stared at a blank screen', body: 'A 500KB JS bundle downloads, Angular boots, builds every DOM node — only <em>then</em> shows content. <strong>4–6 seconds of blank white screen</strong>. 53% of mobile users abandon after 3 seconds.', iconBg: '#fef2f2' },
                { bg: '#fffbeb', border: '#fde68a', icon: '🔍', title: "Google couldn't read Angular apps", body: "Google's crawler sees an empty HTML shell — no content, no metadata. Your pages are <strong>invisible to search engines</strong>. No SEO, no organic traffic.", iconBg: '#fffbeb' },
                { bg: '#f0fdf8', border: '#bbf7d0', icon: '💡', title: 'The insight: move the work from browser → server', body: 'Server runs Angular, builds the full page, sends <strong>ready HTML</strong> to the browser in milliseconds. User sees content immediately. Google crawler sees full content too.', iconBg: '#f0fdf8' },
              ].map(({ icon, title, body, iconBg, border }) => (
                <div class="story-card">
                  <div class="story-icon" style={`background:${iconBg}; border: 1px solid ${border};`}>{icon}</div>
                  <div>
                    <p style="font-size:.82rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--ink-1);margin-bottom:.3rem;">{title}</p>
                    <p style="font-size:.8rem;color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.7;" set:html={body}></p>
                  </div>
                </div>
              ))}
            </div>

            <!-- CSR Timeline -->
            <div class="card" style="margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
              <p style="font-size:.7rem;font-weight:700;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.08em;color:var(--red);margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
                CSR Execution Timeline — What the user experiences
              </p>
              <div class="timeline">
                {[
                  { n:'1', label:'Browser requests page', sub:'Blank screen', time:'0ms', dotCls:'tl-dot-red', timeCls:'tl-time-slow' },
                  { n:'2', label:'Server sends empty HTML', sub:'+ 500KB JS bundle', time:'~50ms', dotCls:'tl-dot-red', timeCls:'tl-time-slow' },
                  { n:'3', label:'Browser downloads JS', sub:'Parsing & executing', time:'~400ms', dotCls:'tl-dot-red', timeCls:'tl-time-slow' },
                  { n:'4', label:'Angular boots', sub:'Modules init', time:'~800ms', dotCls:'tl-dot-red', timeCls:'tl-time-slow' },
                  { n:'5', label:'DOM renders', sub:'User sees content', time:'2–5s', dotCls:'tl-dot-blue', timeCls:'tl-time-ok' },
                ].map(({ n, label, sub, time, dotCls, timeCls }) => (
                  <div class="timeline-step csr">
                    <div class={`tl-dot ${dotCls}`}>{n}</div>
                    <div class="tl-label">{label}</div>
                    <div class="tl-sub">{sub}</div>
                    <span class={`tl-time ${timeCls}`}>{time}</span>
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>
              <p style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--red);font-style:italic;margin-top:.9rem;padding-top:.75rem;border-top:1px solid var(--grid);">
                Google's crawler may give up before step 5. Your page never gets indexed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="card card-warn">
              <p style="font-size:.81rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--ink-1);margin-bottom:.65rem;">🤔 But can't Google just execute JavaScript?</p>
              <p style="font-size:.76rem;color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.75;margin-bottom:.65rem;">
                Google visits your page twice. The <strong style="color:var(--ink-1);">first visit</strong> happens immediately on crawl day —
                Google reads the raw HTML your server sends. If that HTML is an empty shell, Google sees nothing.
                The <strong style="color:var(--ink-1);">second visit</strong> (where Google runs your JavaScript to build the page)
                may happen days or weeks later — or never, for low-traffic pages.
                Your page ranking suffers until that second visit happens.
              </p>
              <p style="font-size:.76rem;color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.75;">
                Worse: <strong style="color:var(--ink-1);">LinkedIn, Twitter, and Facebook never run JavaScript at all</strong> when
                generating link previews. Every link you share shows a blank card — no title, no image, no description.
                SSR fixes both: the server sends complete HTML on the very first response, so every crawler and every
                social media preview works immediately.
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- ── What actually happens inside the browser ── -->
            <p style="font-size:.7rem;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.09em;color:var(--red);text-transform:uppercase;margin:2rem 0 .9rem;">Under the hood — 3 systems inside every browser</p>
            <p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.75;margin-bottom:1rem;">
              To understand why CSR is slow and SSR is fast, you need to know that the browser has
              <strong style="color:var(--ink-1);">three separate systems</strong> — and each one plays a different role in putting pixels on screen.
              People constantly confuse them.
            </p>

            <div class="g3" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
              <div style="background:var(--surface);border:1px solid var(--grid);border-top:3px solid var(--blue);border-radius:.75rem;padding:1rem 1.1rem;">
                <p style="font-size:.65rem;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.08em;color:var(--blue);text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:.5rem;">① HTML Parser</p>
                <p style="font-size:.78rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--ink-1);margin-bottom:.4rem;">Reads HTML text → builds DOM nodes</p>
                <p style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.65;">Runs automatically when HTML arrives over the network. No JavaScript needed. Reads the HTML string character by character and creates a live tree of DOM node objects in memory.</p>
                <p style="font-size:.68rem;color:var(--blue);margin-top:.6rem;font-weight:600;">Triggered by: server sending HTML</p>
              </div>
              <div style="background:var(--surface);border:1px solid var(--grid);border-top:3px solid var(--orange);border-radius:.75rem;padding:1rem 1.1rem;">
                <p style="font-size:.65rem;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.08em;color:var(--orange);text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:.5rem;">② DOM API</p>
                <p style="font-size:.78rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--ink-1);margin-bottom:.4rem;">JavaScript's interface to create/modify DOM nodes &amp; attach events</p>
                <p style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.65;"><code style="font-size:.67rem;background:rgba(0,0,0,.05);padding:.1rem .3rem;border-radius:.25rem;">document.createElement()</code>, <code style="font-size:.67rem;background:rgba(0,0,0,.05);padding:.1rem .3rem;border-radius:.25rem;">appendChild()</code>, <code style="font-size:.67rem;background:rgba(0,0,0,.05);padding:.1rem .3rem;border-radius:.25rem;">addEventListener()</code> — this is how Angular talks to the DOM from JavaScript.</p>
                <p style="font-size:.68rem;color:var(--orange);margin-top:.6rem;font-weight:600;">Triggered by: Angular calling it</p>
              </div>
              <div style="background:var(--surface);border:1px solid var(--grid);border-top:3px solid var(--green);border-radius:.75rem;padding:1rem 1.1rem;">
                <p style="font-size:.65rem;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.08em;color:var(--green);text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:.5rem;">③ Rendering Engine</p>
                <p style="font-size:.78rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--ink-1);margin-bottom:.4rem;">Reads DOM tree → calculates layout → paints pixels</p>
                <p style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.65;">Blink (Chrome), WebKit (Safari), Gecko (Firefox). Watches the DOM for changes and automatically repaints. No JavaScript involved — it runs independently.</p>
                <p style="font-size:.68rem;color:var(--green);margin-top:.6rem;font-weight:600;">Triggered by: DOM tree changing</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div style="background:rgba(0,0,0,.03);border:1px solid var(--grid);border-radius:.75rem;padding:.85rem 1.1rem;margin-bottom:1.5rem;font-size:.72rem;color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.75;">
              <strong style="color:var(--ink-1);">The DOM tree is the shared data structure</strong> all three talk through.
              HTML Parser <em>writes</em> to it. DOM API (Angular) <em>writes</em> to it. Rendering Engine <em>reads</em> from it and paints.
              They never talk to each other directly — they all go through the DOM tree.
            </div>

            <!-- Worker 1 / 2 / 3 deep explanation -->
            <p style="font-size:.7rem;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.09em;color:var(--red);text-transform:uppercase;margin:1.5rem 0 .9rem;">How each worker does its job</p>

            <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:1rem;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">

              <!-- Worker 1 -->
              <div style="background:var(--surface);border:1px solid var(--grid);border-left:3px solid var(--blue);border-radius:.75rem;padding:1rem 1.1rem;">
                <p style="font-size:.68rem;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.08em;color:var(--blue);text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:.5rem;">Worker 1 — HTML Parser</p>
                <p style="font-size:.73rem;color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.75rem;">The server sends a raw text string over HTTP. The HTML Parser reads it character by character. Every time it sees a complete tag, it does one thing: <strong style="color:var(--ink-1);">creates a DOM node object in memory</strong> and places it in the tree. No JavaScript. No painting. Just: text in → DOM nodes out.</p>
                <div class="code-block" style="font-size:.65rem;line-height:1.85;padding:.8rem 1rem;">
                  <span class="cm">// Server sends this raw text string over HTTP:</span><br/>
                  <span class="st">"&lt;h1&gt;Hello&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;World&lt;/p&gt;"</span><br/>
                  <br/>
                  <span class="cm">// HTML Parser reads character by character:</span><br/>
                  <span class="cm">//   sees &lt;h1&gt;  → CREATE h1 node in DOM tree</span><br/>
                  <span class="cm">//   sees Hello → CREATE text node, attach to h1</span><br/>
                  <span class="cm">//   sees &lt;/h1&gt; → close h1</span><br/>
                  <span class="cm">//   sees &lt;p&gt;   → CREATE p node  ...and so on</span>
                </div>
              </div>

              <!-- Worker 2 -->
              <div style="background:var(--surface);border:1px solid var(--grid);border-left:3px solid var(--orange);border-radius:.75rem;padding:1rem 1.1rem;">
                <p style="font-size:.68rem;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.08em;color:var(--orange);text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:.5rem;">Worker 2 — DOM API (JavaScript's writing interface)</p>
                <p style="font-size:.73rem;color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.75rem;">JavaScript cannot touch the DOM tree directly — it goes through the DOM API, a set of browser-provided methods. <strong style="color:var(--ink-1);">Angular is the writer. DOM API is the door. The DOM tree is what changes.</strong></p>
                <div class="code-block" style="font-size:.65rem;line-height:1.85;padding:.8rem 1rem;">
                  <span class="ky">const</span> h1 = document.<span class="fn">createElement</span>(<span class="st">'h1'</span>)    <span class="cm">// CREATE h1 node</span><br/>
                  h1.textContent = <span class="st">'Hello'</span>                    <span class="cm">// attach text node</span><br/>
                  document.body.<span class="fn">appendChild</span>(h1)              <span class="cm">// INSERT into DOM tree</span><br/>
                  <br/>
                  <span class="ky">const</span> btn = document.<span class="fn">createElement</span>(<span class="st">'button'</span>)<br/>
                  btn.<span class="fn">addEventListener</span>(<span class="st">'click'</span>, fn)          <span class="cm">// attach event to node</span><br/>
                  document.body.<span class="fn">appendChild</span>(btn)             <span class="cm">// INSERT into DOM tree</span>
                </div>
              </div>

              <!-- Worker 3 -->
              <div style="background:var(--surface);border:1px solid var(--grid);border-left:3px solid var(--green);border-radius:.75rem;padding:1rem 1.1rem;">
                <p style="font-size:.68rem;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.08em;color:var(--green);text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:.5rem;">Worker 3 — Rendering Engine (Blink in Chrome)</p>
                <p style="font-size:.73rem;color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.75rem;">Watches the DOM tree. The moment any node is added or changed — by either the HTML Parser or by DOM API — it wakes up automatically and runs its 3-step pipeline. <strong style="color:var(--ink-1);">It never talks to JavaScript. It never reads HTML strings. It only reads the DOM tree.</strong></p>
                <div class="code-block" style="font-size:.65rem;line-height:1.85;padding:.8rem 1rem;">
                  <span class="cm">// DOM tree changed (by HTML Parser OR by Angular via DOM API)</span><br/>
                  <span class="cm">// Rendering Engine wakes up automatically:</span><br/>
                  <br/>
                  <span class="ky">1.</span> Style calculation   <span class="cm">→ which CSS applies to each node</span><br/>
                  <span class="ky">2.</span> Layout              <span class="cm">→ where each node sits (x, y, width, height)</span><br/>
                  <span class="ky">3.</span> Paint               <span class="cm">→ draw actual pixels on screen → user sees content</span>
                </div>
              </div>

            </div>

            <!-- CSR vs SSR detailed flow -->
            <p style="font-size:.7rem;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.09em;color:var(--red);text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:.9rem;">How these 3 systems work in CSR vs SSR</p>

            <div class="g2" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;gap:1rem;">

              <!-- CSR flow -->
              <div style="background:var(--surface);border:1px solid var(--grid);border-radius:.75rem;overflow:hidden;">
                <div style="background:#fef2f2;border-bottom:1px solid #fecaca;padding:.65rem 1rem;">
                  <p style="font-size:.7rem;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.07em;color:var(--red);text-transform:uppercase;">CSR — Angular builds the DOM</p>
                </div>
                <div style="padding:.9rem 1rem;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:.6rem;">
                  {[
                    { n:'1', c:'#fef2f2', bc:'#fecaca', nc:'var(--red)', label:'HTML Parser gets empty HTML', body:'Builds skeleton DOM: just <code>&lt;app-root&gt;&lt;/app-root&gt;</code> — no content inside.' },
                    { n:'2', c:'#fef2f2', bc:'#fecaca', nc:'var(--red)', label:'Rendering Engine paints it', body:'Paints the empty skeleton → <strong>blank screen</strong>. User sees nothing.' },
                    { n:'3', c:'#fff7ed', bc:'#fed7aa', nc:'var(--orange)', label:'main.js downloads + executes', body:'<code>defer</code> held it until DOM was ready. Now Angular\'s <code>bootstrapApplication()</code> runs.' },
                    { n:'4', c:'#fff7ed', bc:'#fed7aa', nc:'var(--orange)', label:'Angular creates Component Objects', body:'<code>new HelloComponent()</code> — data and methods live in memory. No DOM yet.' },
                    { n:'5', c:'#fff7ed', bc:'#fed7aa', nc:'var(--orange)', label:'Angular calls DOM API', body:'<code>createElement(\'h1\')</code>, <code>appendChild()</code> — Angular itself builds every DOM node.' },
                    { n:'6', c:'#f0fdf4', bc:'#bbf7d0', nc:'#166534', label:'Rendering Engine repaints', body:'Sees new DOM nodes → paints pixels → user finally sees content. (~2–5s total)' },
                    { n:'7', c:'#f0fdf4', bc:'#bbf7d0', nc:'#166534', label:'Angular attaches event listeners', body:'<code>addEventListener()</code> on the existing DOM nodes. Page is now interactive.' },
                  ].map(({ n, c, bc, nc, label, body }) => (
                    <div style={`display:flex;gap:.65rem;align-items:flex-start;background:${c};border:1px solid ${bc};border-radius:.55rem;padding:.55rem .75rem;`}>
                      <span style={`min-width:1.3rem;height:1.3rem;border-radius:50%;border:1.5px solid ${bc};display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-size:.58rem;font-weight:900;color:${nc};flex-shrink:0;margin-top:.05rem;`}>{n}</span>
                      <div>
                        <p style="font-size:.71rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--ink-1);">{label}</p>
                        <p style="font-size:.68rem;color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.55;margin-top:.15rem;" set:html={body}></p>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  ))}
                </div>
              </div>

              <!-- SSR flow -->
              <div style="background:var(--surface);border:1px solid var(--grid);border-radius:.75rem;overflow:hidden;">
                <div style="background:#f0fdf4;border-bottom:1px solid #bbf7d0;padding:.65rem 1rem;">
                  <p style="font-size:.7rem;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.07em;color:#166534;text-transform:uppercase;">SSR — HTML Parser builds the DOM</p>
                </div>
                <div style="padding:.9rem 1rem;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:.6rem;">
                  {[
                    { n:'1', c:'#eff6ff', bc:'#bfdbfe', nc:'#1d4ed8', label:'Server runs Angular in Node.js', body:'Creates Component Objects, evaluates templates, produces a full HTML string. Sends it over HTTP.' },
                    { n:'2', c:'#f0fdf4', bc:'#bbf7d0', nc:'#166534', label:'HTML Parser gets full HTML', body:'Builds complete DOM: <code>&lt;h1&gt;Hello World&lt;/h1&gt;</code>, all content, fully populated.' },
                    { n:'3', c:'#f0fdf4', bc:'#bbf7d0', nc:'#166534', label:'Rendering Engine paints it', body:'Full DOM → full content on screen <strong>immediately</strong>. User sees page before JS even loads. ✅' },
                    { n:'4', c:'#eff6ff', bc:'#bfdbfe', nc:'#1d4ed8', label:'main.js downloads + executes', body:'Was downloading in parallel (defer). DOM was already ready. <code>bootstrapApplication()</code> runs.' },
                    { n:'5', c:'#eff6ff', bc:'#bfdbfe', nc:'#1d4ed8', label:'Angular creates Component Objects', body:'Fresh <code>new HelloComponent()</code> in browser memory. Server\'s objects are gone — these are new.' },
                    { n:'6', c:'#eff6ff', bc:'#bfdbfe', nc:'#1d4ed8', label:'Hydration — DOM API reads, not writes', body:'Angular does NOT call <code>createElement()</code>. It finds existing nodes and connects them to Component Objects.' },
                    { n:'7', c:'#f0fdf4', bc:'#bbf7d0', nc:'#166534', label:'Angular attaches event listeners', body:'Same as CSR: <code>addEventListener()</code> on existing nodes. Page is now interactive. ✅' },
                  ].map(({ n, c, bc, nc, label, body }) => (
                    <div style={`display:flex;gap:.65rem;align-items:flex-start;background:${c};border:1px solid ${bc};border-radius:.55rem;padding:.55rem .75rem;`}>
                      <span style={`min-width:1.3rem;height:1.3rem;border-radius:50%;border:1.5px solid ${bc};display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-size:.58rem;font-weight:900;color:${nc};flex-shrink:0;margin-top:.05rem;`}>{n}</span>
                      <div>
                        <p style="font-size:.71rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--ink-1);">{label}</p>
                        <p style="font-size:.68rem;color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.55;margin-top:.15rem;" set:html={body}></p>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  ))}
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Key difference callout -->
            <div style="background:rgba(0,0,0,.03);border:1px solid var(--grid);border-radius:.75rem;padding:1rem 1.25rem;margin-bottom:.5rem;">
              <p style="font-size:.72rem;font-weight:800;color:var(--ink-1);margin-bottom:.65rem;">The one difference that explains everything:</p>
              <div class="g2" style="gap:.75rem;">
                <div style="background:#fef2f2;border:1px solid #fecaca;border-radius:.55rem;padding:.75rem 1rem;">
                  <p style="font-size:.68rem;font-weight:800;color:var(--red);margin-bottom:.3rem;">CSR</p>
                  <p style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.6;"><strong style="color:var(--ink-1);">Angular (DOM API)</strong> creates DOM nodes.<br/>Rendering Engine paints them.<br/>User waits for all of this. 😩</p>
                </div>
                <div style="background:#f0fdf4;border:1px solid #bbf7d0;border-radius:.55rem;padding:.75rem 1rem;">
                  <p style="font-size:.68rem;font-weight:800;color:#166534;margin-bottom:.3rem;">SSR</p>
                  <p style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.6;"><strong style="color:var(--ink-1);">HTML Parser</strong> creates DOM nodes from server HTML.<br/>Rendering Engine paints immediately.<br/>Angular only wires events later. ✅</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </section>

