          <!-- ── 02 3 Rendering Modes ── -->
          <section id="rendering-modes" class="section reveal">
            <p class="eyebrow">02 · The Solution</p>
            <h2 class="sec-title">What Are the 3 Ways Angular Can Deliver a Page?</h2>
            <p class="sec-lead">
              The root problem with CSR is that the browser does <em>all</em> the work —
              after a 500KB download, on slow phones, every time. The solution is to move that work
              to a different time or place. Angular offers exactly three options for where and when the page gets built.
            </p>

            <div class="g3">
              <!-- CSR -->
              <div class="mode-card" style="border-top:3px solid var(--orange);">
                <span style="font-size:2rem;line-height:1;">🧑‍🍳</span>
                <span class="mode-badge mtag-csr">CSR — Client-Side Rendering</span>
                <p class="mode-title">The DIY Kitchen</p>
                <p class="mode-desc">Restaurant ships you <strong>raw ingredients + a recipe</strong>. <strong>You cook at home</strong> — food only arrives after you finish cooking.</p>
                <div class="callout callout-amber" style="font-size:.7rem;line-height:1.65;padding:.65rem .9rem;">
                  🥩 JS bundle = ingredients<br/>
                  📋 Angular = recipe<br/>
                  🍳 Browser = your kitchen
                </div>
                <div class="mode-pros-cons">
                  <span><span class="pro">✓</span> Great for dashboards / auth-only</span>
                  <span><span class="pro">✓</span> No server at runtime</span>
                  <span><span class="con">✗</span> Slow first load</span>
                  <span><span class="con">✗</span> Poor SEO</span>
                </div>
