          <!-- ── 05 Hydration ── -->
          <section id="hydration" class="section reveal">
            <p class="eyebrow">05 · Hydration</p>
            <h2 class="sec-title">Why Does Angular Wire Up the Page Instead of Rebuilding It?</h2>
            <p class="sec-lead">
              SSR sends a fully-rendered HTML page. By the time Angular's JavaScript loads,
              that HTML is already visible on screen. This section explains why Angular doesn't
              throw it away and rebuild from scratch — and what it needs from the server to reuse it instead.
            </p>

            <!-- Why Component Objects are created twice -->
            <div style="background:rgba(0,0,0,.03);border:1px solid var(--grid);border-left:3px solid var(--orange);border-radius:.75rem;padding:1rem 1.25rem;margin-bottom:1.75rem;">
              <p style="font-size:.72rem;font-weight:800;color:var(--ink-1);margin-bottom:.85rem;">⚠️ Why Angular creates Component Objects twice</p>
              <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:.6rem;">
                <div style="display:flex;gap:.75rem;align-items:flex-start;background:#fff7ed;border:1px solid #fed7aa;border-radius:.55rem;padding:.65rem .9rem;">
                  <span style="font-size:.85rem;flex-shrink:0;">🖥️</span>
                  <div>
                    <p style="font-size:.71rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--ink-1);margin-bottom:.2rem;">Server — job: produce the HTML string</p>
                    <p style="font-size:.68rem;color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.65;"><code style="font-size:.65rem;background:rgba(0,0,0,.06);padding:.1rem .3rem;border-radius:.25rem;">new HelloComponent()</code> → evaluates template → produces HTML string → <strong style="color:var(--ink-1);">job done → object discarded from RAM</strong>. The HTML string travels over HTTP. The Component Object never does.</p>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div style="display:flex;gap:.75rem;align-items:flex-start;background:#eff6ff;border:1px solid #bfdbfe;border-radius:.55rem;padding:.65rem .9rem;">
                  <span style="font-size:.85rem;flex-shrink:0;">🌐</span>
                  <div>
                    <p style="font-size:.71rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--ink-1);margin-bottom:.2rem;">Browser — job: connect interactivity to existing DOM nodes</p>
                    <p style="font-size:.68rem;color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.65;"><code style="font-size:.65rem;background:rgba(0,0,0,.06);padding:.1rem .3rem;border-radius:.25rem;">new HelloComponent()</code> again — a <strong style="color:var(--ink-1);">fresh object, this time in browser RAM</strong>. The DOM nodes already exist (HTML Parser built them). Angular's only job now is to find them and attach event listeners.</p>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div style="background:rgba(0,0,0,.03);border:1px solid var(--grid);border-radius:.55rem;padding:.55rem .9rem;font-size:.68rem;color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.6;text-align:center;">
                  Component Objects are always created twice — that's unavoidable.<br/>
                  <strong style="color:var(--ink-1);">Hydration stops the DOM from being recreated a second time.</strong> That's its only job.
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
              <p style="font-size:.72rem;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.09em;color:var(--red);text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:.9rem;">
                First: Two Things That Must Both Exist
              </p>
              <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:.85rem;margin-bottom:1rem;">
                <div style="background:linear-gradient(135deg,rgba(234,179,8,.08),rgba(234,179,8,.02));border:1px solid rgba(234,179,8,.22);border-radius:.75rem;padding:1.1rem;">
                  <div style="font-size:1.5rem;margin-bottom:.5rem;">🌳</div>
                  <p style="font-size:.8rem;font-weight:800;color:#854d0e;margin-bottom:.4rem;">DOM Nodes</p>
                  <p style="font-size:.74rem;color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.7;">
                    The browser builds these from the HTML string. They're already on screen — the user sees the content.
                    But they have <strong style="color:var(--ink-1);">zero Angular logic attached</strong>.
                    Click a button right now? Nothing happens. Visible but dead.
                  </p>
                </div>
                <div style="background:linear-gradient(135deg,rgba(221,0,49,.08),rgba(221,0,49,.02));border:1px solid rgba(221,0,49,.22);border-radius:.75rem;padding:1.1rem;">
                  <div style="font-size:1.5rem;margin-bottom:.5rem;">⚙️</div>
                  <p style="font-size:.8rem;font-weight:800;color:#991b1b;margin-bottom:.4rem;">Component Objects</p>
                  <p style="font-size:.74rem;color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.7;">
                    Angular creates these in the browser's RAM when its JS bundle loads.
                    They hold all the logic: click handlers, <code>ngOnInit</code>, services.
                    But on their own they have <strong style="color:var(--ink-1);">no DOM presence</strong> — just objects in memory.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="callout" style="background:rgba(99,102,241,.07);border:1px solid rgba(99,102,241,.2);font-size:.76rem;">
                <p class="callout-title" style="color:#818cf8;">💡 The connection problem</p>
                The server's HTML became DOM nodes. Angular created component objects.
                These two things need to be <strong>connected</strong> — each component object wired to the DOM node it controls.
                That connection is what makes the dead HTML interactive. This process is called <strong>hydration</strong>.
              </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Part B: 3 steps -->
            <div style="margin-bottom:2rem;">
              <p style="font-size:.72rem;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.09em;color:var(--red);text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:.9rem;">
                What Actually Happens — 3 Steps
              </p>
              <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:.5rem;margin-bottom:1rem;">

                <div style="display:flex;gap:1rem;align-items:flex-start;background:var(--surface);border:1px solid var(--grid);border-radius:.65rem;padding:.85rem 1rem;">
                  <div style="min-width:1.7rem;height:1.7rem;border-radius:50%;background:#eff6ff;border:1px solid #bfdbfe;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-size:.65rem;font-weight:900;color:#1d4ed8;flex-shrink:0;">1</div>
                  <div>
                    <p style="font-size:.76rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--ink-1);margin-bottom:.2rem;">Browser parses HTML → DOM nodes appear on screen</p>
                    <p style="font-size:.73rem;color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.65;">Content is visible immediately — fast first paint. But nothing is clickable. Angular's JS hasn't loaded yet.</p>
                  </div>
                </div>

                <div style="display:flex;gap:1rem;align-items:flex-start;background:var(--surface);border:1px solid var(--grid);border-radius:.65rem;padding:.85rem 1rem;">
                  <div style="min-width:1.7rem;height:1.7rem;border-radius:50%;background:#fff7ed;border:1px solid #fed7aa;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-size:.65rem;font-weight:900;color:#c2410c;flex-shrink:0;">2</div>
                  <div>
                    <p style="font-size:.76rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--ink-1);margin-bottom:.2rem;">Angular JS bundle loads — component objects created in browser RAM</p>
                    <p style="font-size:.73rem;color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.65;">Angular boots fresh. It creates component objects — but does <em>not</em> recreate the DOM. It needs to find the existing DOM nodes and connect to them. How does it know which node belongs to which component?</p>
                  </div>
                </div>

                <div style="display:flex;gap:1rem;align-items:flex-start;background:var(--surface);border:1px solid var(--grid);border-radius:.65rem;padding:.85rem 1rem;">
                  <div style="min-width:1.7rem;height:1.7rem;border-radius:50%;background:#f0fdf4;border:1px solid #bbf7d0;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-size:.65rem;font-weight:900;color:#166534;flex-shrink:0;">3</div>
                  <div>
                    <p style="font-size:.76rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--ink-1);margin-bottom:.2rem;">Angular reads the ng-state map → wires component objects to DOM nodes</p>
                    <p style="font-size:.73rem;color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.65;">The server embedded a map in the HTML: "node:3 = ProductListComponent." Angular reads it, finds that DOM node, connects its component object to it, attaches click handlers. The page is now interactive.</p>
                  </div>
                </div>

              </div>

              <div class="callout callout-amber" style="font-size:.76rem;">
                <p class="callout-title" style="color:#92400e;">🔥 Why the ng-state map matters</p>
                Without it, Angular would have to re-render every component from scratch to figure out which DOM nodes it produced —
                re-running <code>ngOnInit</code>, re-fetching data, re-building everything. That defeats the entire purpose of SSR.
                The map is the server saying: <em>"here's which node is which — don't redo my work."</em>
              </div>
            </div>

